# POPEN(3S)

## NAME

popen, pclose - initiate I/O to/from a process

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <stdio.h>

FILE *popen(const char *command, const char *type);

int pclose(FILE *stream);
```

## DESCRIPTION

The arguments to *popen* are pointers to null-terminated strings containing respectively a
shell command line and an I/O mode, either "r" for reading or "w" for writing. It creates a
pipe between the calling process and the command to be executed. The value returned is a stream
pointer that can be used (as appropriate) to write to the standard input of the command or read
from its standard output.

A stream opened by *popen* should be closed by *pclose,* which waits for the associated process
to terminate and returns the exit status of the command.

Because open files are shared, a type "r" command may be used as an input filter, and a type
"w" as an output filter.

## SEE ALSO

pipe(2), fopen(3), fclose(3), system(3), wait(2)

## DIAGNOSTICS

*Popen* returns a null pointer if files or processes cannot be created, or the Shell cannot be
accessed.

*Pclose* returns -1 if *stream* is not associated with a `popened` command.

## BUGS

Buffered reading before opening an input filter may leave the standard input of that filter
mispositioned. Similar problems with an output filter may be forestalled by careful buffer
flushing, e.g. with *fflush,* see fclose(3).

## BESM-6 NOTES

*Pclose* returns wait(2)'s raw status, and **that status is only 15 bits wide here:** it comes
back in r12, an index register, and a v7 status is (code << 8), which passes 32767 as soon as
the exit code passes 127. Exit codes 128..255 therefore arrive truncated. See wait(2).

*Popen* and *pclose* are declared in <stdio.h> rather than at their own file head, which is
unusual for a routine no v7 header declared: it is done because a *caller* needs them, and this
is the only place in the library where a **FILE \*** and a fork(2) meet.

One v7 bug is fixed rather than carried: a *fork* that fails now closes *both* ends of the pipe
before returning NULL. v7 leaked them, so a program that retried on failure ran out of
descriptors.

